WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.040 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Walk through downtown Chicago today, and
almost nothing at street level tells

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:07.740 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
you that another transportation network
once

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:11.060 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
existed roughly forty feet below the
traffic.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:12.850 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Not a subway.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:14.580 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
A freight railroad.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:21.660 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Small electric locomotives hauled coal,
merchandise, supplies, and ash through

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:26.060 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
narrow tunnels beneath the Loop. The
tracks connected with railroad freight

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:31.700 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
houses. In some buildings, they reached
directly into basement-level facilities.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:35.180 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
And this was not one tunnel linking two
points.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:38.520 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
At its height, the network stretched
roughly sixty

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:40.540 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
miles beneath downtown Chicago.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:45.940 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
For decades, freight moved through an
underground grid while the city continued

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:51.500 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
above it. Then, in July 1959, the trains
stopped.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.240 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The railroad disappeared as a working
system.

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:57.220 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The tunnels did not.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:02.620 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
So why did Chicago need an entire freight
railroad beneath its streets?

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:06.260 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
And how could infrastructure this
extensive become

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.400 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
obsolete while much of its

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:09.996 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
physical footprint remained?

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.900 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The reason begins with a problem Chicago
could see every day.

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:17.980 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Around the turn of the twentieth century,
the Loop was packed with

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:23.280 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
competing forms of movement. Railroad
terminals, warehouses, department stores,

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:28.480 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
offices, light industry, streetcars,
pedestrians, horse-drawn wagons, and

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:33.160 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
deliveries were all trying to use the same
limited street grid.

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:37.140 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Freight did not simply arrive in Chicago
and stop.

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:40.316 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
It had to make the final trip to the
customer.

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:46.840 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Stores needed merchandise. Buildings
needed supplies. Boiler rooms needed coal.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:51.080 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
And once that coal was burned, the ash had
to be removed.

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:55.060 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Every one of those movements consumed
street space.

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:58.590 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Chicago did not have a transportation
shortage.

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:01.560 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
It had a transportation concentration
problem.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:05.327 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The surface was carrying too many
different jobs at once.

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:09.900 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
That made the space beneath the streets
valuable for a reason that

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:14.020 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
had nothing to do with passengers. If some
of the city’s repetitive

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:18.420 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
freight movements could be pushed
underground, the same downtown could keep

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:23.760 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
functioning without asking every wagon and
delivery to fight for the same curb.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:27.600 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The idea was practical because the problem
was physical.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:32.620 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
But the system that emerged from that
problem had an unexpected origin.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:37.940 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Chicago’s underground freight railroad did
not actually begin as a railroad.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:44.040 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
In 1899, an independent telephone company
received a franchise connected to

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.580 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
putting telephone infrastructure
underground.

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:49.980 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Tunnel construction began around 1900.

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:55.327 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Those tunnels were large enough to become
something more than simple cable conduits.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:58.700 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Exactly when the freight-railway idea
became

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:01.600 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
the real objective is not completely
clear.

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:06.400 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The surviving evidence does not justify
the neat story that a railroad

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:09.120 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
had secretly been the plan from the first
day.

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:12.200 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
What is clear is the transformation.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:18.600 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Track was installed on a two-foot gauge.
Small electric locomotives could operate

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:22.740 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
in spaces far too small for conventional
railroad equipment.

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:26.800 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
By 1906, regular freight service had
begun.

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:31.760 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
A project associated with underground
communications infrastructure had evolved

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:33.700 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
into an urban freight system.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.880 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
That solved one problem and immediately
created another.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:44.520 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Chicago’s mainline railroads used normal
freight cars. The tunnel railroad used

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:48.100 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
tiny cars running through confined
underground passages.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:51.349 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The two systems could connect
commercially.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:53.609 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
They could not connect physically.

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:58.640 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Forty feet beneath downtown Chicago,
freight moved through a railroad that

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:00.980 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
ordinary railroad cars could never enter.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:04.580 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
That meant transfer was built into the
system.

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:09.080 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
A shipment could arrive at one of
Chicago’s surface railroad freight houses,

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:12.900 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
then be transferred into much smaller
tunnel cars.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:17.260 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Those cars could be lowered to the
underground level and moved behind

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:19.460 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
compact electric locomotives.

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:24.050 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
From that point on, the freight was
traveling through a second railroad.

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:27.680 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The surface railroad had brought it to
downtown Chicago.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:32.320 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The tunnel railroad handled the next stage
beneath the streets.

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:37.540 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
And in some cases, that underground stage
ended inside the customer’s building.

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:43.320 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Connections and sidings reached basement-
level facilities, allowing freight to

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:47.740 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
move from the tunnel network into
buildings without making the last trip

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:49.280 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
across crowded streets.

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:53.440 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
That arrangement sounds elaborate because
it was elaborate.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:58.860 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Freight had to change vehicles. The
underground cars were small. The system

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:01.780 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
required elevators, shafts, track,
electrical

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:04.060 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
equipment, and building connections.

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:07.940 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Every transfer added handling. Every
elevator

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:11.240 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
and customer connection added fixed
infrastructure.

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:15.360 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The tunnel railroad therefore had to earn
its place not by being

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:20.660 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
simpler than street delivery, but by being
useful enough in a congested

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:23.480 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
downtown to justify that complexity.

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:26.860 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The complexity only had to beat the
alternative.

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:32.740 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
If moving freight across downtown streets
was difficult enough, transferring it

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:34.960 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
underground could still make sense.

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:39.500 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
And once Chicago committed to the idea, it
did not build a

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:41.260 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
small demonstration line.

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:46.520 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The easiest way to underestimate Chicago’s
freight tunnels is to picture one

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:48.620 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
long underground passage.

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:50.440 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
This was a network.

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:55.100 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
By the time the system had matured, track
spread beneath much of

00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:58.800 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
downtown in a grid that followed the
streets above.

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:02.640 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The total network reached roughly sixty
miles.

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:05.380 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
That scale changes the story.

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:11.000 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
This was not simply a tunnel between one
railroad terminal and one large customer.

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:16.680 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
It was infrastructure intended to serve
many freight movements across the Loop.

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:22.660 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Connections linked the underground system
with railroad freight houses. Commercial

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:27.800 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
buildings could connect from above.
Beneath the streets, small electric

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:33.340 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
locomotives moved through a city most
people experienced only at street level.

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:40.760 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
By 1910, contemporary regulatory reporting
described nearly sixty miles of track,

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:46.560 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
connections to twenty-two railroad freight
houses, and service to forty-five

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:48.020 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
commercial buildings.

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:54.350 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
By 1924, the number of commercial-building
connections had grown to about sixty

00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:58.660 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
even as overall traffic was already
showing signs of decline.

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:04.160 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Those numbers matter because they show
what the system really was: not

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:06.440 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
a novelty tunnel, but an operating

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:09.580 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
distribution network with customers,
transfer

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:13.420 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
points, and a large fixed footprint
beneath the Loop.

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:16.260 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The contrast was almost absurd.

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:20.120 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Chicago was one of America’s defining
railroad cities.

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:25.500 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
And underneath it was a second railroad
built to a two-foot gauge,

00:07:25.540 --> 00:07:29.980 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
using machines small enough to fit through
tunnels that would have been

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:32.240 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
useless to a conventional locomotive.

00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:36.760 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
A very large network depended on very
small trains.

00:07:37.340 --> 00:07:41.320 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
But its most convincing use case was not
glamorous freight.

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:47.200 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
It was one of the most repetitive material
cycles in an industrial city.

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:49.250 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Coal went in.

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:50.740 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Ash came back out.

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:55.140 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Coal explains why the tunnel railroad
could make economic sense.

00:07:55.740 --> 00:07:59.440 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Large downtown buildings needed fuel for
their boiler rooms.

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:04.740 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Delivering that fuel at street level meant
bringing heavy material into an

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:06.940 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
already congested district and then

00:08:06.980 --> 00:08:09.380 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
getting it from the curb into the
building.

00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:12.940 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
An underground connection changed that
geometry.

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:18.560 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Coal could travel beneath the street and
reach basement-level facilities without

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:21.460 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
adding another delivery wagon to the
traffic above.

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:25.940 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Then, after the coal was burned, the waste
had to leave.

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:29.260 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The tunnel system could carry ash back
out.

00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:30.850 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Coal in.

00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:32.030 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Ash out.

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:35.480 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The same fixed network could serve both
directions

00:08:35.480 --> 00:08:39.260 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
of a repetitive, heavy, dirty logistics
problem.

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:43.660 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The railroad also carried merchandise,
packages,

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:46.380 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
building materials, and other general
freight.

00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:50.700 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
But coal and ash reveal the deeper
business logic.

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:55.530 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The system was not trying to be a
glamorous replacement for passenger rail.

00:08:55.760 --> 00:09:00.100 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
It was trying to make the hidden physical
work of downtown more efficient.

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:05.280 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
For a building connected to the network,
that could be a compelling arrangement.

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:07.453 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
And that phrase matters:

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:10.240 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
for a building connected to the network.

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:16.120 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Because Chicago could build sixty miles of
track beneath downtown and still

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:19.340 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
face a much smaller problem at the end of
every route.

00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:26.340 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Chicago’s tunnel railroad had one major
weakness: every useful delivery still

00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:28.880 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
needed a physical connection to the
customer.

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:33.140 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
A tunnel could run beneath a block and
still be useless to

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:34.902 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
the building directly above it.

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:37.960 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The customer needed a way into the
network.

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:43.140 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
That meant additional construction,
additional expense, and a permanent

00:09:43.180 --> 00:09:45.620 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
relationship with fixed infrastructure.

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:50.140 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The system’s scale did not eliminate that
final problem.

00:09:50.740 --> 00:09:55.120 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
In some cases, building owners bore the
cost of making those connections.

00:09:55.680 --> 00:10:00.120 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
And a surface delivery vehicle offered a
brutally simple alternative.

00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:02.380 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
It could stop at the curb.

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:07.480 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
It did not require the customer to
excavate a private underground connection.

00:10:08.020 --> 00:10:12.700 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
It did not care whether the building had
been designed around the tunnel system.

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:17.200 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
So the railroad’s greatest strength was
also one of its constraints.

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:20.660 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
It was embedded in the geography of
downtown.

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:26.100 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
That was valuable when freight patterns
were predictable, customers stayed in

00:10:26.140 --> 00:10:31.720 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
place, terminals remained where the
network expected them to be, and buildings

00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:35.920 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
continued to need the kinds of deliveries
the tunnels handled well.

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:41.640 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
But fixed infrastructure becomes much less
forgiving when those conditions move.

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:44.060 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The system had solved Chicago’s

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:47.260 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
street problem by becoming physically
committed to

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:48.820 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
one version of the city.

00:10:49.580 --> 00:10:51.780 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Then the city started changing around it.

00:10:51.820 --> 00:10:56.740 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
By the 1930s, the tunnel railroad was
facing a new kind of competition.

00:10:57.620 --> 00:10:59.180 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Trucks did not need rails.

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:01.436 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
They did not need elevators.

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:06.090 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
They did not need a building to pay for a
dedicated underground connection.

00:11:06.380 --> 00:11:10.240 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
They could follow the street network
almost anywhere, and they could follow

00:11:10.280 --> 00:11:12.740 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
customers when those customers moved.

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:16.220 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
That flexibility mattered because
Chicago’s

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:18.560 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
freight geography was changing too.

00:11:19.180 --> 00:11:23.860 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Railroad freight facilities shifted away
from some of the locations the tunnel

00:11:23.900 --> 00:11:26.120 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
system had been built to serve.

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:29.500 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Some light industrial activity left the
Loop.

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:32.460 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The relationship between downtown
buildings and

00:11:32.500 --> 00:11:34.840 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
surface transportation was changing.

00:11:35.340 --> 00:11:38.660 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
It is tempting to compress all of this
into one sentence:

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:41.640 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
trucks killed the tunnel railroad.

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:43.600 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
That is too simple.

00:11:44.420 --> 00:11:48.440 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Trucks were one factor in a much broader
loss of fit between

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:51.200 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
the network and the city around it.

00:11:51.440 --> 00:11:53.820 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The locomotives could still move.

00:11:54.400 --> 00:11:56.440 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The tracks could still carry cars.

00:11:57.160 --> 00:11:59.580 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The problem was that the economic system

00:11:59.620 --> 00:12:02.640 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
that made those movements valuable was
changing.

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:08.540 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
And then Chicago began building a newer
underground railroad for an entirely

00:12:08.580 --> 00:12:09.630 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
different purpose.

00:12:10.780 --> 00:12:15.336 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Chicago’s passenger subway arrived while
the freight tunnels were still operating.

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:20.800 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Beginning around 1939, construction of the
subway at the same general underground

00:12:20.840 --> 00:12:24.540 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
level cut important parts of the older
freight network.

00:12:25.380 --> 00:12:28.520 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
One generation of transportation
infrastructure was

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:30.480 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
physically disrupting another.

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:35.520 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
At the same time, the buildings above were
changing their own relationship

00:12:35.560 --> 00:12:36.860 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
with the tunnel system.

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:42.460 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Coal deliveries became less important as
buildings moved away from coal and

00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:44.700 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
eventually toward natural gas.

00:12:45.460 --> 00:12:47.040 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Freight terminals moved.

00:12:48.180 --> 00:12:49.736 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Industrial activity changed.

00:12:50.240 --> 00:12:51.796 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Trucks became more flexible.

00:12:52.340 --> 00:12:55.820 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
And parts of the underground network were
no longer as useful or

00:12:55.860 --> 00:12:58.240 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
as connected as they had once been.

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:00.840 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
No single switch was thrown.

00:13:01.460 --> 00:13:05.050 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
There was no moment when the technology
suddenly stopped functioning.

00:13:05.240 --> 00:13:09.200 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The railroad was losing the ecosystem that
justified it.

00:13:09.580 --> 00:13:11.580 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
That is a different kind of failure.

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:16.300 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
A machine can become obsolete because a
better machine replaces it.

00:13:16.920 --> 00:13:21.410 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Infrastructure can become obsolete because
the pattern of life around it changes.

00:13:21.940 --> 00:13:27.560 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
By the 1950s, Chicago’s tunnel railroad
was increasingly optimized for a downtown

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:29.120 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
that was disappearing.

00:13:29.980 --> 00:13:33.840 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
In July 1959, freight operations ended.

00:13:34.580 --> 00:13:37.802 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The electric locomotives stopped running
through downtown.

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:43.760 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The underground cars stopped carrying
coal, ash, merchandise, and supplies.

00:13:44.740 --> 00:13:47.080 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
A transportation system that had once

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:50.040 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
extended roughly sixty miles beneath
Chicago

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:52.620 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
ceased to function as a railroad.

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:56.880 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
But a railroad and a tunnel are not the
same thing.

00:13:57.520 --> 00:13:59.140 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The business could end.

00:13:59.620 --> 00:14:01.240 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The traffic could end.

00:14:01.760 --> 00:14:04.038 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The tracks and equipment could disappear.

00:14:04.340 --> 00:14:07.500 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
And the physical void beneath the city
could remain.

00:14:08.340 --> 00:14:11.740 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
That distinction is the hinge in this
story.

00:14:12.740 --> 00:14:16.780 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
By 1959, Chicago’s underground freight
trains were gone.

00:14:17.600 --> 00:14:19.540 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The tunnels were still there.

00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:24.380 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
After closure, the old tunnel system did
not have one single fate.

00:14:24.980 --> 00:14:26.700 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Some sections were cut off.

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:29.020 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Some were filled or altered.

00:14:29.680 --> 00:14:30.940 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Some remained empty.

00:14:31.720 --> 00:14:34.553 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Parts were reused for utilities and
communications.

00:14:35.120 --> 00:14:39.620 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The important point is not that the city
somehow forgot the tunnels existed.

00:14:40.040 --> 00:14:41.050 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
It did not.

00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:43.920 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The important point is that they no longer

00:14:43.960 --> 00:14:47.080 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
functioned as one coherent freight
business.

00:14:47.660 --> 00:14:50.440 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Above ground, Chicago kept evolving.

00:14:51.320 --> 00:14:54.080 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Below ground, remnants of the old network

00:14:54.120 --> 00:14:57.160 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
became part of the city’s physical
inheritance.

00:14:58.060 --> 00:15:02.220 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
And physical networks have a property
business networks do not.

00:15:02.820 --> 00:15:05.340 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
A business connection can disappear on
paper.

00:15:06.080 --> 00:15:10.060 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
A physical connection can remain open in
concrete and earth.

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:15.440 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
If a pathway still exists, something can
still move through it.

00:15:16.020 --> 00:15:20.120 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
More than three decades after the last
freight train ran, Chicago would

00:15:20.160 --> 00:15:22.500 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
get a dramatic demonstration of that fact.

00:15:23.140 --> 00:15:29.200 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
On April 13, 1992, water from the Chicago
River began pouring into

00:15:29.240 --> 00:15:31.000 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
the old freight tunnel system.

00:15:31.540 --> 00:15:35.600 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The breach occurred where the tunnel
network passed beneath the river near

00:15:35.640 --> 00:15:37.080 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
the Kinzie Street area.

00:15:38.020 --> 00:15:41.640 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Once the water entered, it found the
legacy of the freight railroad

00:15:41.680 --> 00:15:42.900 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
waiting for it:

00:15:43.240 --> 00:15:46.380 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
a connected underground pathway through
downtown.

00:15:47.400 --> 00:15:51.320 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The same basic quality that had made the
system valuable for freight

00:15:51.360 --> 00:15:55.500 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
now made it consequential for an entirely
different reason.

00:15:56.480 --> 00:16:00.702 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Water spread through the old tunnels and
into connected downtown structures.

00:16:00.880 --> 00:16:04.020 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Basements and sub-basements took on water.

00:16:04.420 --> 00:16:05.809 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Utilities were disrupted.

00:16:06.600 --> 00:16:11.720 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The U.S. Geological Survey later reported
that utility services were lost in

00:16:11.760 --> 00:16:14.580 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
more than one hundred downtown buildings.

00:16:15.380 --> 00:16:19.660 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Different sources describe the number of
directly flooded or affected buildings

00:16:19.700 --> 00:16:25.260 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
differently, so the event does not reduce
cleanly to one building count.

00:16:26.040 --> 00:16:28.560 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The mechanism is clearer than the
accounting.

00:16:28.940 --> 00:16:33.980 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
A network built to distribute freight was
still physically connected enough to

00:16:34.020 --> 00:16:35.240 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
distribute water.

00:16:35.620 --> 00:16:40.060 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Pile-driving work near the Kinzie Street
Bridge had taken place before the

00:16:40.100 --> 00:16:45.600 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
failure, and later legal claims alleged
that this work had damaged or

00:16:45.640 --> 00:16:46.751 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
weakened the tunnel.

00:16:47.420 --> 00:16:51.760 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
But the legal history does not support
simplifying that into a claim

00:16:51.800 --> 00:16:55.700 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
that a contractor was definitively found
responsible for the flood.

00:16:56.160 --> 00:16:59.840 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The larger point is more important to this
story anyway.

00:17:00.700 --> 00:17:04.033 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The freight railroad had been closed for
thirty-three years.

00:17:04.680 --> 00:17:09.120 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Its physical connectivity could still
shape what happened in downtown Chicago.

00:17:09.900 --> 00:17:12.440 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The flood did not bring the railroad back.

00:17:12.880 --> 00:17:16.180 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
It exposed something more useful for
understanding cities:

00:17:16.900 --> 00:17:21.860 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
a system can vanish from everyday economic
life without vanishing from the

00:17:21.900 --> 00:17:23.456 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
physical logic of the place.

00:17:24.080 --> 00:17:29.540 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The tunnel network no longer had trains,
customers, or a freight business.

00:17:30.100 --> 00:17:31.620 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
But it still had geometry.

00:17:32.360 --> 00:17:33.582 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
It still had passages.

00:17:34.360 --> 00:17:37.880 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
And where those passages remained
connected, they could still transmit

00:17:37.920 --> 00:17:41.060 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
consequences from one part of downtown to
another.

00:17:41.840 --> 00:17:46.580 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The flood revealed the difference between
infrastructure that stops operating and

00:17:46.620 --> 00:17:49.020 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
infrastructure that stops existing.

00:17:49.840 --> 00:17:54.420 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Four years after the flood, one surviving
piece of the railroad came

00:17:54.460 --> 00:17:55.640 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
back into view.

00:17:56.420 --> 00:18:02.240 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Locomotive Number 508 had remained
underground with four ash cars on an

00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:05.880 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
isolated section of the old system near
the Field Museum.

00:18:06.900 --> 00:18:12.800 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
In 1996, construction associated with Lake
Shore Drive exposed the equipment.

00:18:13.460 --> 00:18:15.340 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The locomotive was recovered.

00:18:15.860 --> 00:18:20.440 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Today, Number 508 is preserved by the
Illinois Railway Museum.

00:18:21.340 --> 00:18:26.160 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
It is a remarkably small physical survivor
of a very large system.

00:18:27.060 --> 00:18:32.040 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Built in 1908, Number 508 is only about
eleven feet long and

00:18:32.080 --> 00:18:33.900 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
a little over five feet high.

00:18:34.580 --> 00:18:37.540 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
That compact size was not a curiosity.

00:18:38.360 --> 00:18:41.040 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
It was dictated by the railroad around it.

00:18:41.380 --> 00:18:46.380 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The tunnels demanded small equipment, and
the small equipment in turn shaped

00:18:46.420 --> 00:18:49.020 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
how freight had to be transferred and
handled.

00:18:49.960 --> 00:18:54.780 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
For decades, machines like it moved
freight through tunnels beneath one of

00:18:54.820 --> 00:18:57.080 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
America’s defining railroad cities.

00:18:58.140 --> 00:19:01.700 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Seeing the locomotive changes the scale of
the story again.

00:19:02.440 --> 00:19:04.320 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The map becomes a machine.

00:19:05.180 --> 00:19:11.100 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The abstract network becomes steel,
wheels, motors, and a cab small enough

00:19:11.140 --> 00:19:12.190 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
to fit the tunnel.

00:19:13.180 --> 00:19:17.620 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Chicago’s underground freight railroad was
not an unrealized plan.

00:19:18.280 --> 00:19:21.336 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
It was not a legend about a lost city
beneath the city.

00:19:21.860 --> 00:19:22.910 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
It operated.

00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:26.800 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
And one of its locomotives is still here
to prove it.

00:19:27.680 --> 00:19:30.640 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Chicago’s tunnel railroad did not
disappear because

00:19:30.680 --> 00:19:32.940 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
one invention suddenly made it useless.

00:19:33.880 --> 00:19:36.980 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Its world changed piece by piece.

00:19:37.520 --> 00:19:39.900 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Surface delivery became more flexible.

00:19:40.460 --> 00:19:41.904 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Freight geography shifted.

00:19:43.040 --> 00:19:44.762 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Customers and industries moved.

00:19:45.500 --> 00:19:48.556 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Passenger subway construction cut parts of
the network.

00:19:48.980 --> 00:19:51.869 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Buildings stopped depending on coal in the
same way.

00:19:52.440 --> 00:19:55.400 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Each change weakened the logic of
infrastructure

00:19:55.440 --> 00:19:58.300 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
built around fixed tracks, fixed

00:19:58.340 --> 00:20:03.800 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
terminals, fixed building connections, and
a particular pattern of downtown commerce.

00:20:04.620 --> 00:20:08.740 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
By 1959, the freight railroad was finished
as a business.

00:20:09.440 --> 00:20:12.760 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The tunnels were not finished as physical
objects.

00:20:13.260 --> 00:20:14.540 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Some remained useful.

00:20:15.220 --> 00:20:16.270 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Some were altered.

00:20:16.640 --> 00:20:17.690 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Some were filled.

00:20:18.420 --> 00:20:22.320 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Some became empty spaces beneath a city
that had moved on.

00:20:22.980 --> 00:20:29.040 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
And in 1992, the old network demonstrated
that infrastructure can remain

00:20:29.080 --> 00:20:33.580 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
consequential long after its original
business model is gone.

00:20:34.480 --> 00:20:37.258 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
That may be the most important part of
this story.

00:20:38.300 --> 00:20:42.680 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Chicago did not simply build a strange
railroad and later abandon it.

00:20:43.000 --> 00:20:48.140 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
It built a transportation system optimized
for a specific version of the city.

00:20:49.020 --> 00:20:52.280 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
For a time, that system solved a real
problem.

00:20:53.140 --> 00:20:57.420 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Then the city changed faster than the
infrastructure beneath it could.

00:20:58.360 --> 00:21:01.460 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Today, traffic still fills the Loop.

00:21:01.500 --> 00:21:05.220 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Railroads still define Chicago. New
buildings

00:21:05.260 --> 00:21:09.440 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
still rise above streets that have been
rebuilt again and again.

00:21:10.360 --> 00:21:14.180 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
And beneath parts of that modern city are
remnants of an older

00:21:14.220 --> 00:21:16.320 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
answer to an older problem.

00:21:17.040 --> 00:21:18.480 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The railroad vanished.

00:21:19.160 --> 00:21:21.040 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
The infrastructure did not.

00:21:21.980 --> 00:21:25.580 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Infrastructure can outlive the economy
that created it.

00:21:25.700 --> 00:21:27.140 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Sometimes by decades.

00:21:27.840 --> 00:21:30.720 line:68% position:50% align:center size:88%
Sometimes long enough to matter all over
again.

